@startuml cowrie-session-lifecycle-P0-FAULT-POINTS
' =============================================================================
' STATUS: VERIFIED_AS_DEPLOYED (post P0 recovery, 2026-09-23)
'
' Cowrie 2.5.0 post-authentication session lifecycle, annotated with the
' three P0 defects found on 2026-09-23 and their exact failure points.
'
' Provenance: evidence/2026-09-23-p0-recovery/ (SHA256SUMS-manifested).
' Every fault point below is backed by a captured traceback, not inferred.
'
' WHY THIS DIAGRAM EXISTS: this layer is strictly UPSTREAM of the H2
' enrichment pipeline. H2 consumes cowrie.command.input. If this lifecycle
' breaks after login.success, H2 has nothing to enrich -- and every
' service-level health check still passes. The H2 diagrams show what happens
' to telemetry once it exists; this one shows whether it exists at all.
' =============================================================================

title Cowrie Session Lifecycle — P0 Fault Points (VERIFIED_AS_DEPLOYED 2026-09-23)

skinparam sequenceMessageAlign left
skinparam maxMessageSize 220

participant "Attacker / client" as C
participant "HoneyPotSSHTransport" as T
participant "UserAuthServer" as A
participant "CowrieSSHConnection\n/ HoneyPotSSHSession" as S
participant "shell/server.py\ninitFileSystem" as SRV
participant "insults.py\nLoggingServerProtocol" as I
participant "cowrie.json\n(telemetry)" as J

== Transport establishment ==

C -> T : TCP connect
T -> J : cowrie.session.connect
C -> T : SSH version exchange
T -> J : cowrie.client.version
C -> T : KEXINIT / host-key algorithm negotiation
T -> T : privHostKey.sign(exchangeHash,\n  signatureType=self.keyAlg)

note right of T #FFCCCC
  **P0A FAULT POINT** — transport.py:1585 / :1690
  Ed25519 key material occupied the rsa_* slot.
  Client negotiating ssh-rsa =>
    BadSignatureAlgorithmError
  Transport dies HERE. No auth, no shell.
  Survival depended on the client's chosen
  algorithm — which is why the failure looked
  intermittent rather than total.
  STATUS: FIXED (type-correct slots;
  Ed25519 fingerprint preserved)
end note

T -> J : cowrie.client.kex

== Authentication ==

C -> A : userauth (password)
A -> J : **cowrie.login.success**
note right of A
  Everything above this line worked even
  while the sensor was producing zero
  intelligence. 16,070 logins reached
  exactly this point and no further.
end note

== Session channel ==

C -> S : channel open "session"
S -> J : (channel open)
C -> S : request_env (LANG=...)
S -> J : cowrie.client.var

== Shell / exec request — where it actually died ==

C -> S : request_exec("whoami")  /  request_shell + pty-req
S -> SRV : execCommand -> initFileSystem(home)
SRV -> SRV : HoneyPotFilesystem(arch, home)
SRV -> SRV : getCommandOutput(\n  CowrieConfig.get("shell","processes"))

note right of SRV #FFCCCC
  **P0B1 FAULT POINT** — server.py:72 / :83
  [shell] processes = 5  ->  open("5")
    FileNotFoundError
  Only NoOptionError is caught, so this
  escapes initFileSystem and surfaces as
  SSH_MSG_CHANNEL_FAILURE.
  Runs on FIRST exec/shell request, not at
  startup — so the service boots clean.
  STATUS: FIXED (-> cmdoutput.json, absolute)
end note

SRV -> I : protocol.makeConnection(processprotocol)
I -> I : connectionMade()\nttylog.ttylog_open(ttylogFile, startTime)

note right of I #FFCCCC
  **P0B2 FAULT POINT** — insults.py:69 -> ttylog.py:27
  /opt/cowrie/var/lib/cowrie/tty was
  root:cowrie 0750; cowrie user had no write.
    PermissionError [Errno 13]
  connectionMade runs UPSTREAM of the
  exec/interactive split (insults.py:47-50),
  so it killed BOTH modes identically.
  STATUS: FIXED (chown cowrie:cowrie)
end note

== Post-repair: telemetry finally produced ==

S -> J : **cowrie.session.params**
S -> J : **cowrie.command.input**  input='whoami'
I -> I : ttylog_close -> os.rename(file, <input-hash>)\n(insults.py:214-221, also needs dir write)
S -> J : cowrie.log.closed
T -> J : cowrie.session.closed

note over J #CCFFCC
  These four events are what the H2
  enrichment pipeline consumes.
  Before 2026-09-23 they were never emitted:
  16,070 login.success / 0 command.input.
  2025 control baseline for comparison:
  80.1% post-auth command rate,
  median login -> first command ~0.464s.
end note

note across #FFFFCC
  COMMON FAILURE CHARACTER: all three defects allowed Cowrie to boot, bind
  its listener, complete KEX, authenticate, open a channel, and accept
  environment variables. Each failed only at the moment an attacker tried to
  act. No service-level health check — systemd state, port binding, auth
  success rate — could detect any of them. Detection required either
  post-auth telemetry rate monitoring or an active end-to-end shell probe.
end note

@enduml
